eed one more. Maybe they can form a pair from another tile. But they don't have any other tiles. Hmm. Maybe they can't form Seven Pairs. So perhaps they need to form two chows or pungs.\n\nLooking at the tiles again: W2, W3, W7x2, W9x2, B3, B4.\n\nIf they can form a chow with W2, W3, and W4. But they don't have W4. So no. Alternatively, W7, W8, W9. They have W7 and W9, but no W8. So no. \n\nAlternatively, maybe they can form a pung of W7 or W9. If they can get another W7 or W9, but they have to play one first. If they play W7, others might take it. Similarly for W9.\n\nAlternatively, maybe they can form a chow with B3, B4, B5. They have B3 and B4, but need B5. B5 has 4 remaining. If they can draw a B5, they can form that chow. But if they play B4 now, someone else might take it for a chow.\n\nSo, considering the possible plays:\n\nIf they play W2 or W3, they might not be as critical as B4 or W9. Playing B4 could help others form a chow. Playing W9 might be a terminal, but if someone is waiting for it, it's risky.\n\nLooking at the unshown tiles:\n\nW7 has 4 remaining. If they play W7, others might need it for a chow (like W6, W7, W8). They have W6 in the unshown count (3 left). So if someone has W6 and W8, they could take W7. But W8 has 3 left. Not sure.\n\nW9 has 3 remaining. If someone is building a terminal hand, they might need W9. But since it's a terminal, it's more likely to be part of a pair or a pung. They have two W9s, so if they play one, others might need it for a pair.\n\nB4 has 4 remaining. If someone has B3 and B5, they could take it for a chow. They have B3 in hand, so playing B4 might help others.\n\nConsidering the game history, the next player has played several tiles, including T7, T9, etc. The player across has some melds. The previous player has played W5, which is in the unshown count (3 left). \n\nAnother angle: their hand might be aiming for a hand with multiple pungs or kongs. For example, if they can form a pung of W7 or W9, that would add